[SIZE="4"][B]Source code[/B][/SIZE]

[LIST]
[*][URL="https://github.com/chijure-android-system-development/android_device_huawei_y220/tree/cm-10.1"]Huawei Y220 device tree[/URL] (branch cm-10.1; README.md explains every piece)
[*][URL="https://github.com/chijure-android-system-development/android_kernel_huawei_y220-u05"]Huawei Y220-U05 kernel[/URL]
[*][URL="https://github.com/chijure-android-system-development/android_vendor_huawei_y220/tree/cm-10.1"]Huawei Y220 vendor[/URL] (branch cm-10.1; 4.2.2 components from the Y330-U01 B121 firmware, modem and firmware from the Y220 C482B206SP05)
[*]CyanogenMod cm-10.1, unmodified
[/LIST]

[SPOILER="Technical notes"]
[LIST]
[*][B]UBIFS:[/B] a small static helper attaches system, userdata and cache before init mounts them. CyanogenMod's generic init.rc mounts mtd@system, mtd@cache and mtd@userdata as yaffs2, which erases UBI partitions on this NAND, so the device tree ships its own init.rc without those lines.
[*][B]MediaTek blobs:[/B] the symbols they expect from MediaTek's own liblog, libcutils, libjpeg, libmedia, libui and libcamera_client come from a shim library loaded through LD_PRELOAD.
[*][B]NVRAM:[/B] the Y330 and Y220 NVRAM tables number the audio files differently, so the application side of NVRAM comes from the Y330 and the modem side (IMEI) from the Y220.
[*][B]Audio:[/B] a wrapper loads the stock MediaTek HAL (its module id is not the one CyanogenMod expects) and converts the 48 kHz stereo microphone input to what AudioFlinger asks for. The audio policy is CyanogenMod's.
[*][B]Calls:[/B] the Y330 audio HAL sends the modem two speech settings the older Y220 modem does not accept, and the modem crashed at the start of every call. The wrapper drops one (the Enh1 downlink gain) and keeps the voice volume above zero for the other.
[*][B]Camera:[/B] a wrapper hides the analog TV pseudo camera, removes HDR, limits picture sizes to the sensor's 640x480 and passes on the JPEG that MediaTek sends in its own message. For video it translates MediaTek's name for its frame format so Android's recorder accepts it. The camera tuning library is the Y220's own (gc0329 sensor).
[*][B]Wi-Fi:[/B] wpa_supplicant 8 over nl80211; the wlan_mt6582 module carries a kernel fix (set_default_key) so WPA works.
[*][B]Hotspot:[/B] the kernel switches the MediaTek driver to AP mode when Android asks for it and registers the AP as wlan0, where hostapd and the tethering expect it. IPv6 is now on in the kernel, which Android 4.2 needs for tethering.
[*][B]Modem recovery:[/B] MediaTek restarts a crashed modem from user space, but the process that asks for it cannot do so under CyanogenMod's init permissions. A small service in the device tree watches the modem state and asks for the restart itself.
[*][B]RIL:[/B] MediaTekRIL turns the radio on the way MediaTek's framework does (the plain Android request lost a race with the SIM check at boot), and handles the MediaTek URC numbering, incoming call indication, GPRS attach and the MediaTek signal strength format.
[*][B]Bluetooth:[/B] Bluedroid with a vendor library over the stock MediaTek driver library.
[/LIST]
[/SPOILER]

